---
import MaterialPage from "@/components/MaterialPage.astro";
import { materialBySlug } from "@/data/materials";

const entry = materialBySlug("channel-split");
if (!entry) throw new Error("Unknown material");
---

<MaterialPage entry={entry}>
  <p slot="intro">
    <code>ChannelSplitMaterial</code> takes the red, green and blue values of every pixel in your
    text and lets them drift apart. You have seen the effect on an old CRT, on a worn VHS tape, and
    in the coloured fringe where a projector’s light meets a wall. Green stays put; red and blue
    slide away from it in opposite directions along an axis you choose.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="uOffset">
    <p>How far the red and blue channels travel from the green one, along the axis set by <code>uRotation</code>.</p>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="uOffset-value">
    <p>A float from <code>0.0</code> to <code>1.0</code>, as a fraction of the text’s total height, padding and line height included.</p>
    <p>The blur behind this effect has an internal 200-pixel cap, so on a 400-pixel-tall text an offset of <code>1.0</code> renders as <code>0.5</code> would.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="uRotation">
    <p>The angle, in degrees, along which the channels split.</p>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="uRotation-value">
    <p>A float from <code>0.0</code> to <code>360.0</code>.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="uApplyBlur">
    <p>A switch. When on, a motion blur is drawn across the distance set by <code>uOffset</code>, so the channels smear into one another rather than sitting as three clean copies. Softer, ghostlier.</p>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="uApplyBlur-value">
    <p>A float used as a boolean: <code>0.0</code> for off, <code>1.0</code> for on.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="uAnimateNoise">
    <p>The blur samples the channels through a little noise, partly for speed and partly because it looks right. When this switch is on, that noise animates and the smear crackles like static.</p>
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="uAnimateNoise-value">
    <p>A float used as a boolean: <code>0.0</code> for off, <code>1.0</code> for on. Only matters while <code>uApplyBlur</code> is on.</p>
  </Fragment>
</MaterialPage>
